course: dictionary
lesson: 624
title: Baboon: a monkey, and a rude word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A baboon is a large monkey from Africa and Arabia. It has a dog-like face, long jaws and big teeth, and it lives in a group called a troop. When you meet a word with more than one meaning, read the words around it to see which meaning fits.
explain: «The baboons marched along the road, and the whole troop stopped for a drink.» Road, troop and drink tell you that baboon means an animal here.
explain: Sometimes a word is used in a rude way. If someone shouts, «You baboon, that was a silly thing to do!», the word is an insult. Calling someone a baboon means they are acting rough or silly, and it is rude, so do not say it to people.
fact: A baboon is a large monkey from Africa and Arabia, with a dog-like face.
fact: Baboons live in large groups called troops, and they spend much of their time on the ground.
fact: In ancient Egypt, Hamadryas baboons were sacred, which means they were treated as very special.
fact: «Baboon» can also be an informal insult: calling someone a baboon means they are acting rough or silly, and it is rude.
fact: In parts of South Africa, baboons steal food from people and cars, so never feed them.
exercise: In the sentence «Baboons live in large groups called troops», what is a troop? | A small boat | *A large group of animals that live together | A type of hornet's nest | A shout at dawn
exercise: According to science lessons, where do baboons usually live? | Only high in the trees of Europe | Under the sea in coral reefs | Inside people's houses in London | *On the ground in savannas and rocky hills
exercise: «Calling someone a baboon is rude.» What does this sentence tell you about the word baboon? | It means someone is very clever | It is a kind of food | *It can be an insult | It is the name of a town
test: In the story, the wise Baboon does not answer the hungry Leopard's question. What does he give them instead? | *Riddles about "spots" and "change" | A map of the veldt | A big dinner | A new house
test: «The baboons marched along the road.» What does marched suggest? | Danced wildly | *Walked together in a steady, orderly line | Flew over the hills | Slept under a tree
exam: Which statement about baboons is true? | *In parts of South Africa, baboons may steal food, so do not feed them | Calling someone a baboon is a kind compliment | Baboons live only in water | Baboons are small birds that fly over the sea
